+### Parameters and Performance Constants
+The computational cost $T_{WWMC}$ is defined as [S1]:
+$$ T_{WWMC} = T_I(numC) + T_O(numAC) + K_{CC} \times numC + K_{ACT} \times numAC $$
+Where:
+*   $numC$: Total number of cells in the dataset [S1].
+*   $numAC$: Number of active cells [S1].
+*   $T_I(numC)$: I/O time for reading input cells [S1].
+*   $T_O(numAC)$: I/O time for writing output triangles [S1].
+*   $K_{CC}$: Constant time cost for Cell Classification per cell. Measured at $8.36 \times 10^{-8}$ sec on the test workstation [S1].
+*   $K_{ACT}$: Constant time cost for Active Cell Triangulation per active cell. Varies between $3.1 \times 10^{-7}$ sec and $5.7 \times 10^{-7}$ sec, depending on the number of triangles produced [S1].
+
+### Pitfalls and Edge Cases
+*   **Topological Inconsistency**: The standard lookup table causes holes/cracks on shared faces due to ambiguous cases. WWMC uses a modified lookup table to ensure consistent triangulations [S1].
+*   **Redundant Computation**: Without deduplication, shared vertices are recalculated up to four times. WWMC computes vertices once and stores indices [S1].
+*   **Memory Limits**: Datasets larger than RAM require dynamic buffering. Buffer sizes must be tuned to $N_S \le Z_{max}$ based on available RAM [S1].
+*   **Precision**: Linear interpolation assumes distinct vertex values. If $V_a \approx V_b$, division by zero or numerical instability may occur. A fallback (e.g., midpoint) is required [G].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Parallel Marching Cubes (General Framework)
-A parallelization strategy for WWMC that subdivides cells among several parallel processes, leveraging the independence of cell treatment [S1]. The data unit for partitioning is a pair of slices, corresponding to $(X_{max}-1) \times (Y_{max}-1)$ cells, which keeps I/O overheads low and permits fine-grain partitions [S1]. A key pitfall is the duplication of vertices lying on shared slices between consecutive intervals across processes; triangles with one or two shared vertices are considered 'shared triangles' and require careful management to avoid topological inconsistencies [S1]. The goal of spatial subdivision is to ensure each process receives a part of the volume with approximately the same number of triangles and low amounts of duplicated vertices [S1].
+A parallelization strategy for WWMC that subdivides cells among several parallel processes, leveraging the independence of cell treatment [S1]. The data unit for partitioning is a pair of slices, corresponding to $(X_{max}-1) \times (Y_{max}-1)$ cells, which keeps I/O overheads low and permits fine-grain partitions [S1]. A key pitfall is the duplication of vertices lying on shared slices between consecutive intervals, leading to "shared triangles" (triangles with one or two shared vertices) that require careful management to avoid topological inconsistencies across processes [S1]. The goal of spatial subdivision is to balance the workload (number of triangles) while minimizing duplicated vertices [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time Complexity:** The computational cost is modeled as:
-$$ T_{WWMC} = T_I(numC) + T_O(numAC) + K_{CC} \times numC + K_{ACT} \times numAC $$
-where $T_I$ and $T_O$ are I/O times, $numC$ is the total number of cells, $numAC$ is the number of active cells, $K_{CC}$ is the classification cost per cell, and $K_{ACT}$ is the triangulation cost per active cell [S1]. The execution time is proportional to the size of the input dataset ($numC$) and the size of the isosurface ($numAC$) [S1].
-
-**Memory Complexity:** Memory usage is dominated by the Vertex Table, Triangle Table, and the auxiliary Vertex Index Map. The size of the Vertex Table is proportional to the number of unique vertices on the isosurface. The algorithm uses dynamic buffering to handle datasets larger than main memory, with buffer sizes determined by available RAM [S1].
-
-**Performance:** On a workstation, $K_{CC}$ is constant at $8.36 \times 10^{-8}$ sec, while $K_{ACT}$ varies between $3.1 \times 10^{-7}$ sec and $5.7 \times 10^{-7}$ sec [S1]. The Active Cell Triangulation operation costs approximately one magnitude order more than the Cell Classification operation [S1].
-
-**Strengths and Limitations:**
-*   **Strengths:** Efficiently treats arbitrary datasets and isosurfaces; handles large datasets via I/O buffering; avoids topological inconsistencies; reduces redundant computation via vertex deduplication [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Execution time is high because it is proportional to the size of the input dataset. It is less efficient than search structures for multiple queries on the same dataset, as search structures incur setup overhead but reduce execution time for subsequent queries [S1]. WWMC is most efficient when the dataset is queried only once or a few times [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity**: The execution time is proportional to the size of the input dataset ($numC$) and the size of the isosurface ($numAC$) [S1]. Specifically, $T_{WWMC} \approx K_{CC} \cdot numC + K_{ACT} \cdot numAC$ for large datasets where I/O is overlapped or negligible relative to computation, though I/O is explicitly modeled [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity**: Depends on the number of unique vertices and triangles. Vertex deduplication reduces the size of the Vertex Table compared to naive implementations [S1]. I/O buffering requires memory proportional to $N_S \times X_{max} \times Y_{max} \times B$ [S1].
+*   **Performance**: On the test workstation, $K_{CC}$ is significantly lower than $K_{ACT}$ ($8.36 \times 10^{-8}$ vs $3.1-5.7 \times 10^{-7}$ sec) [S1]. In many datasets, less than 10% of cells are active, making the classification step relatively cheap compared to triangulation [S1].
+*   **Strengths**: Efficiently handles arbitrary datasets and isosurfaces; resolves topological inconsistencies; manages memory for large datasets via buffering; reduces redundant computation [S1].
+*   **Limitations**: Execution time is high for dense datasets because it is proportional to the total number of cells $numC$ [S1]. Search structures (pre-computed) may be more efficient for multiple queries on the same dataset, but WWMC is more efficient for single or few queries [S1].
+*   **When to Use**: Single-pass or few-pass extraction of large volumetric datasets that exceed main memory, where topological correctness is critical [S1].
+*   **When Not to Use**: When multiple queries on the same dataset are required (search structures are better) or when the dataset is small enough that overhead of buffering/deduplication is not justified [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The Watt & Watt Marching Cubes (WWMC) algorithm was introduced by D. D’Agostino, A. Clematis, and V. Gianuzzi in their 2006 paper "Parallel Isosurface Extraction for 3D Data Analysis Workflows in Distributed Environments" [S1]. The algorithm addresses issues identified in the original Lorensen and Cline Marching Cubes proposal, incorporating solutions from prior literature: the modified lookup table from [25] for topological consistency, and the vertex deduplication technique from [27] for performance [S1]. It is used in medical image processing, fluid dynamics simulations, and bioinformatics applications where efficient isosurface extraction from large volumetric datasets is required [S1].
+WWMC was introduced by D. D'Agostino, A. Clematis, and V. Gianuzzi in their work on parallel isosurface extraction for 3D data analysis workflows [S1]. The algorithm addresses four specific issues identified in the original Lorensen and Cline Marching Cubes proposal: topological inconsistencies, performance overhead from non-active cells, redundant vertex calculation, and memory management for large datasets [S1]. The first three issues were included in the version proposed in [27] (referenced in [S1]), and the I/O buffering was added to handle datasets larger than main memory [S1]. The name "Watt & Watt" is attributed to the inclusion of optimizations from [27], which itself builds upon earlier work [S1].
 
